<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lahijan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lahijan is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> province in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. From the 14th to the 16th century it was the capital of the Karkiya dynasty, which sheltered the young Safavid leader Ismail there in the 1490s. In the 20th century it became the centre of Iranian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> growing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lahijan.jpg" alt="Lahijan" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammad Azizi · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lahijan,_11_September_2011_16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lahijan is a city near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> in the Central District of Lahijan County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> province, of which it is the capital. It stands on the northern slope of the Alborz Mountains at an altitude of 94 metres, west of Langarud and east of Astaneh-ye Ashrafiyeh, and its foundations rest on sediments laid down by the rivers of Gilan, among them the Sefid-Rud. It has a humid Hyrcanian climate with warmer winters and cooler summers than most of Gilan. The city had 19,877 inhabitants at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1956 and 101,073 in 2016, making it the third largest city in the province after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-e-anzali">Bandar-e Anzali</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s present provincial divisions the Sefid-Rud divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> into east and west, and Lahijan became capital of the eastern part, called Biehpish. The region has long been a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> production. Tradition attributes the city's foundation to Lahij ebne Saam. The Mongol ruler Oljaito conquered it in 705 AH, and Amir Teimoor attacked the region. Plague broke out in 703 and a fire in 850, and the Russian army occupied Lahijan in June 1725. It was also one of the main bases of the Jungle Movement.</p><p>Lahijan was the first place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>. Prince Mohammad Mirza, known as Kashef-ol-Saltaneh, who was born in Lahijan and served as the first mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and as ambassador to British-ruled India, posed as a French labourer to learn the secrets of tea production and brought about 3,000 saplings from the Kangra region of northern India, using his diplomatic immunity to avoid scrutiny. A first attempt with imported Indian seed in 1882 had failed. The first modern tea factory opened in 1934, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> hills came to hold about 107 factories and 32,000 hectares of tea. Kashef's mausoleum in Lahijan is part of the Iran National Tea Museum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lahijan blends traditional and modern architecture and is a tourist destination in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its sights include the Lahijan Pool, a reservoir of 17 hectares with an island that tradition says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> meant for a palace and the tomb of Sheikh Zahed Gilani, grandmaster of the Zahediyeh Sufi Order. Lahijan is known for the kulucheh, a filled cookie, and for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a> ceremonies, in which long reed trumpets called karna are played in villages such as Mashk, Lasht and Rudbeneh.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lahijan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sayyid Ali Kiya, a Zaydi sayyid supported by the Mar'ashi dynasty of Mazandaran, took <b>Lahijan</b> and eastern Gilan (Biyapish) around 1370.</blockquote><a href="/state/karkiya-dynasty">Karkiya dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
